ProteoSync is an automated sequence alignment tool that allows the easy creation of customizable sequence alignment and projection of conservation onto 3D surfaces.

----------------------
~ SETUP INSTRUCTIONS ~
----------------------

STEP 1: Downloading the database files with git-lfs

The database files are hosted via git-lfs, and must be fetched separately after cloning the main repo.

To get the dataset files, first install git-lfs. They have several official installers on their website.
Once you have cloned the git repository to your computer and git-lfs is properly installed, in the terminal, cd into the cloned repository, then run git lfs pull. This will download all of the necessary dataset files.

STEP 2: Installing required python packages

The program utilizes several python packages that must be installed.

This can be done using the included requirements file. In the console, cd to the ProteoSync directory and run:
	pip install -r requirements.txt

STEP 3: Installing external executables with Anaconda

The program calls on a few external programs, such as BLAST and clustalw, which need to be installed separately. This should be done using a free program called Conda.

    - First, install Conda at https://www.anaconda.com/products/distribution
    - Then, open the terminal and install the packages by running the following lines:
        conda install -c conda-forge dssp
        conda install -c bioconda clustalw
        conda install -c bioconda blast

----------------------
~ USAGE INSTRUCTIONS ~
----------------------

To run the program, run the main.py file included in the package.

Understanding the Menu

“Protein sequence” box: This is where you enter the sequence you want to search. To search sequences in bulk, separate sequences with whitespace (space or newline)

“UniProt ID” box: Alternatively, instead of entering a protein sequence, you can enter the UniProt ID of the protein to be analyzed. The sequence will be downloaded from UniProt for analysis. If an AlphaFold prediction for the protein is available from UniProt, it will be downloaded and included in the structural alignment. Any input in this box overrides all input in the protein sequence box.

“Search PDB database?” checkbox: If you don’t want the program to add PDB structures to the final alignment, you can uncheck this box. PDB search is enabled by default.

“% Identity threshold” slider: You can adjust this slider to set the threshold for which species are included in the final result based on their similarity to the query. Any species whose closest sequence has % identity to the query that is lower than the threshold are excluded from the final alignment.

“% Length variability” slider: You can adjust this slider to set the threshold for which sequences are filtered based on length difference to the query. The threshold applies to % length difference in both directions; e.g. a length threshold of 40% will filter out sequences that are less than 60% or greater than 140% of the length of the query.

“Output file name” box: If you want the output file to have a specific name, you can enter it here. If left empty, a generic name will be generated.

“Update PDB database” button: Click this button to update the local PDB sequence database. Updating the database is not necessary for the program to function, but it’s helpful to have the most recent structures on hand. Update as frequently as you see fit.

“Taxonomy Settings” menu: This window allows you to handpick which species you want included in your search. All species in the database are organized by taxonomy, with checkboxes allowing you to enable or disable species or taxonomic groups. If you want to add a new species, see the database instructions document in the instructions folder.

“Color Settings” menu: This window allows you to select a color scheme for the 3D PyMOL projection.


Interpreting Your Results

Each output file contains a sequence alignment of all sequences found within the identity and length thresholds set by the user.

To the left of each sequence is the name of the species the sequence belongs to, followed by % identity to the query sequence.

Below the sequences are the structures of similar proteins found in the PDB database that have been overlaid onto the sequence alignment. Each structure line is labelled on the left with its PDB code. This section only appears if PDB search is enabled.
The structure is represented by a string of characters as follows:
	•	‘A’ - this residue is part of an alpha helix
	•	‘B’ - this residue is part of a beta sheet
	•	‘-‘ - this residue is unstructured
	•	‘X’ - this residue is not modelled in this PDB structure

Below the PDB structures, the structure of the AlphaFold prediction for the protein is overlaid onto the sequence alignment. It is represented by a string of characters, going by the same legend as the PDB structures. This section only appears if a UniProt ID for the query sequence is entered.

Below the AlphaFold structure are clustalw conservation scores representing conservation across the aligned sequences.
clustalw conservation scores are as follows:
	•	‘*’ - the residue is identical across all aligned sequences
	•	‘:’ - conserved substitutions exist between aligned species
	•	‘.’ - semi-conserved substitutions exist between aligned species
	•	‘ ‘ - this residue is not conserved

At the bottom of the alignment, accession codes for all aligned species and a list of all species outside the user-set thresholds are listed.


Using the PyMOL output file

Along with each alignment, the program creates a file labelled with ‘_pymol’ in the file name. This is a PyMOL command file that will project the conservation scores from the corresponding alignment onto a 3D structure in PyMOL.

To use the file, first open a PDB model of the protein in PyMOL. An AlphaFold structure prediction or a full-sequence solved structure from the PDB will work. Be warned that any added or removed residues in the .pdb file can offset the color alignment. Thus for solved structure files from the PDB, it is good to ensure that there are no unmodelled sections or extraneous tags, and that the sequence starts counting at 1.

Then, in the command bar, type @, then drag the output file into the command bar. This should fill in the file path to the command file. Then press enter, and the commands will execute to colour the model.

The darker the colour, the more conserved that residue is. White means completely unconserved, and the darkest blue means completely identical.